tel 1.0

base orthodoxy
import soundness

info   OAuth 2 for clients and web applications: grants, PKCE, scoped tokens and typed credentials
detail
    _Orthodoxy_ implements [OAuth 2](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6749), the protocol by
    which a client obtains an access token from an API's authorization server and presents it
    with each request, on a user's behalf or on its own. A token is more than a string — it
    grants particular scopes, it expires, and it may come with a refresh token — so it is an
    `Authorization` carrying all four, against which a `Scope` can be checked.

    An `Issuer` is an authorization server as one client sees it, and each grant is a method
    on it which posts to the token endpoint through the `Http.Client` in scope and raises
    `OAuth.Error` on refusal:

    ```scala fuel
    typecheck
    ##
    import soundness.*
    import strategies.throwUnsafely
    import internetAccess.online
    import httpBackends.javaNetHttp
    import logging.silentLogging

    val issuer = Issuer(url"https://issuer.example/token", t"client-1", t"s3cret")

    val token: Authorization = issuer.clientCredentials(t"read", t"write")
    token.grants(List(t"read"))
    url"https://api.example/items".fetch(authorization = token).receive[Text]
    ```

    For the authorization-code flow, `Issuer#authorizationUrl` builds the URL a user is sent
    to, with a `Pkce` challenge, and `Issuer#exchangeCode` exchanges the code they bring back.
    A `Credential` names a security scheme as an API's specification names it, and is how an
    OpenAPI client from apoplexy finds the key or token each operation requires.

    The library has two source sets:

    - `orthodoxy.core` — `Issuer`, `Authorization`, `Pkce`, `Scope`, `Credential` and
      `OAuth.Error`: everything a client needs.
    - `orthodoxy.server` — the authorization-code flow as scintillate middleware, the
      extensions `oauth` and `require` on `Issuer`, with `Authorizations` holding each
      session's state.

    Everything is exported to the `soundness` umbrella. There are no choice packages of its
    own, but the grants need the usual contextual values from elsewhere: an `Online` token
    (`internetAccess.online`), an HTTP backend (`httpBackends.javaNetHttp`), a
    `Http.Event is Loggable` (`logging.silentLogging`), and a `Tactic[OAuth.Error]`, each
    imported by name.

refer orthodoxy.Issuer
refer orthodoxy.Authorization
refer orthodoxy.OAuth.Error
refer orthodoxy.oauth

entry .Issuer   an OAuth 2 authorization server as one client sees it: its endpoints and the client's identity
  detail
      An `Issuer` holds the token endpoint, `#exchange`; the client's identity, `#client`,
      and its `#secret` if it has one; and, for the authorization-code flow, the
      authorization endpoint, `#init`, and the `#redirect` URI registered for the client.
      Its grant methods are the client side of RFC 6749: each posts to the token endpoint,
      with the client's identity and secret as form parameters (RFC 6749 §2.3.1's
      `client_secret_post`), and reads the `Authorization` the server returns.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import internetAccess.online
      import httpBackends.javaNetHttp
      import logging.silentLogging

      val issuer = Issuer(url"https://issuer.example/token", t"client-1", t"s3cret")

      val token: Authorization = issuer.clientCredentials(t"read", t"write")
      val fresh: Authorization = issuer.refresh(token)
      ```

      Every grant needs an `Online` token, a `Http.Event is Loggable`, an `Http.Client` for
      `http` or `https` origins (an HTTP backend such as `httpBackends.javaNetHttp`), and a
      `Tactic[OAuth.Error]`. A `400` or `401` from the token endpoint is
      `OAuth.Error.Reason.Unauthorized`, a connection failure `Connection`, a response which
      does not parse `InvalidJsonResponse`, and any other status `UnexpectedHttpStatus`. A
      method which needs `#init` or `#redirect` when the issuer was built without it raises
      `Misconfigured`.
  refer orthodoxy.Authorization
  refer orthodoxy.Pkce
  refer orthodoxy.OAuth.Error
  entry .Context   evidence, in a request handler, that it runs inside one issuer's `oauth` middleware
    detail
        `Issuer.Context of issuer.type` is provided by `Issuer#oauth` to the handler it wraps,
        and required by `Issuer#require`, so that the redirect `require` sends a user on is
        one the same issuer's `oauth` will handle when the user returns. It carries nothing
        but its `Topic` type.
    entry .apply   makes the context for an issuer's singleton type, as `Issuer.Context[issuer.type]()`
  entry #exchange   the token endpoint, to which every grant posts
  entry #client   the client's identifier, sent as `client_id`
  entry #secret   the client's secret, sent as `client_secret`, or `Unset` for a public client
  entry #init   the authorization endpoint a user is sent to, or `Unset` if the authorization-code flow is not used
  entry #redirect   the redirect URI registered for the client, or `Unset` if the authorization-code flow is not used
  entry #grant   posts a token request with arbitrary grant parameters and reads the `Authorization`
      The common step of every grant: the given `Query` of parameters is posted to
      `#exchange` with `client_id`, and `client_secret` where there is one. For a grant type
      this class has no method for, pass its parameters here.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import internetAccess.online
      import httpBackends.javaNetHttp
      import logging.silentLogging

      val issuer = Issuer(url"https://issuer.example/token", t"client-1", t"s3cret")
      issuer.grant(Query(List(t"grant_type" -> t"password", t"username" -> t"ada", t"password" -> t"…")))
      ```
  entry #clientCredentials   the client-credentials grant: the client's own authorization, for the given scopes
      RFC 6749 §4.4. The scopes are sent space-separated as `scope`, and omitted when none are
      given, in which case the server grants its default.
  entry #refresh   a new authorization from a refresh token, or from an authorization which carries one
      RFC 6749 §6. Takes the refresh token as `Text`, or an `Authorization`, whose `#refresh`
      token is used; an authorization with no refresh token raises
      `OAuth.Error.Reason.Unauthorized` without contacting the server.
  entry #exchangeCode   exchanges an authorization code for an authorization, with the PKCE verifier if one was used
      RFC 6749 §4.1.3. Posts the `code` the user brought back to `#redirect` with the
      redirect URI and, when a `Pkce` is passed, its verifier (RFC 7636), which must be the
      same `Pkce` whose challenge went into `#authorizationUrl`. Raises
      `OAuth.Error.Reason.Misconfigured` if the issuer has no `#redirect`.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import internetAccess.online
      import httpBackends.javaNetHttp
      import logging.silentLogging

      val flow = Issuer
        ( url"https://issuer.example/token", t"client-1", Unset,
          url"https://issuer.example/authorize", url"https://app.example/callback" )

      val pkce = Pkce()
      val code: Text = t"…"   // from the redirect's `code` parameter
      flow.exchangeCode(code, pkce)
      ```
  entry #authorizationUrl   the URL a user is sent to, to authorize the client for some scopes
    detail
        RFC 6749 §4.1.1. The URL is `#init` with `client_id`, `redirect_uri`,
        `response_type=code`, the `state` (which the redirect back must echo, and which the
        application should check), the scopes space-separated, and, when a `Pkce` is passed,
        its `S256` challenge. It makes no request, so it needs only a
        `Tactic[OAuth.Error]`, raised as `Misconfigured` if the issuer has no `#init` or no
        `#redirect`.

        ```scala fuel
        frame
          label Scopes and state
          code
              import soundness.*
              import strategies.throwUnsafely

              val flow = Issuer
                ( url"https://issuer.example/token", t"client-1", Unset,
                  url"https://issuer.example/authorize", url"https://app.example/callback" )

              flow.authorizationUrl(List(t"read"), t"xyz").show
              // https://issuer.example/authorize?client_id=client-1
              //   &redirect_uri=https%3A%2F%2Fapp.example%2Fcallback&response_type=code&state=xyz
              //   &scope=read
        frame
          label With a PKCE challenge
          code
              import soundness.*
              import strategies.throwUnsafely

              val flow = Issuer
                ( url"https://issuer.example/token", t"client-1", Unset,
                  url"https://issuer.example/authorize", url"https://app.example/callback" )

              val pkce = Pkce(t"dBjftJeZ4CVP-mB92K27uhbUJU1p1r_wW1gFWFOEjXk")
              flow.authorizationUrl(List(t"read"), t"xyz", pkce).show
              // … &scope=read
              //   &code_challenge=E9Melhoa2OwvFrEMTJguCHaoeK1t8URWbuGJSstw-cM&code_challenge_method=S256
        ##
        ```

entry .Authorization   an access token and what it grants: its scopes, its expiry and a refresh token
  detail
      What a token response grants (RFC 6749 §5.1): the access token itself, `#key`; the
      `#scopes` granted; the `#expiry` instant; and the `#refresh` token, if one was issued.
      Every grant on `Issuer` returns one, and `.parse` reads one from a token response's
      JSON.

      An `Authorization` is passed directly as an HTTP request's `authorization` header,
      through the companion's `.authorization` directive, rendered as `Bearer <key>`:

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import internetAccess.online
      import httpBackends.javaNetHttp
      import logging.silentLogging

      val issuer = Issuer(url"https://issuer.example/token", t"client-1", t"s3cret")
      val token = issuer.clientCredentials(t"read")

      val current = if token.expired then issuer.refresh(token) else token
      url"https://api.example/items".fetch(authorization = current).receive[Text]
      ```

      A token whose response named no scopes is taken to grant exactly those requested, as
      RFC 6749 §5.1 has it, so `#grants` is `true` for any scopes when `#scopes` is empty.
      `Authorization of scope`, a refinement of its `Topic`, is an authorization known to
      grant a `Scope`: `Scope#apply` and `Issuer#require` produce one.
  refer orthodoxy.Scope
  refer orthodoxy.Issuer
  entry .authorization   sets a request's `authorization` header from an `Authorization`, as `Bearer <key>`
      The telekinesis `Directive` for the `authorization` named argument of `fetch` and
      `submit`, in the companion and so in implicit scope.
  entry .parse   reads an `Authorization` from the JSON of a token response
      Reads `access_token` (required), `scope` (split on spaces; absent means `Nil`),
      `expires_in` (seconds, converted to an instant in milliseconds from now) and
      `refresh_token`. A response with no `access_token` raises
      `OAuth.Error.Reason.InvalidJsonResponse` through the `Tactic[OAuth.Error]` in scope.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val json = t"""{"access_token": "tok", "expires_in": 3600, "scope": "read write"}""".as[Json]
      Authorization.parse(json).scopes   // List(t"read", t"write")
      ```
  entry #key   the access token itself
  entry #scopes   the scopes granted, or `Nil` if the response did not name them
  entry #expiry   when the token expires, in milliseconds since the epoch, or `Unset` if no lifetime was stated
  entry #refresh   the refresh token, or `Unset` if none was issued
  entry #bearer   the token as an `Auth.Bearer`, as the `Authorization` header carries it
  entry #expired   whether the token's expiry has passed; a token with no stated lifetime never expires
  entry #grants   whether the token grants every one of the given scopes
      `true` when `#scopes` contains them all, and also whenever `#scopes` is empty, since a
      response which named no scopes granted those requested.

entry .Scope   a named set of OAuth scopes, which checks that an authorization grants them
  detail
      A `Scope(t"read", t"write")` names the scopes an operation needs. Applied with `()`, it
      checks the `Authorization of …` given in scope and returns it refined as
      `Authorization of scope.type`, so code which demands that refinement can only be
      reached once the check has passed. Each missing scope is raised as
      `OAuth.Error.Reason.InsufficientPrivileges`.

      `Issuer#require(read, write)` provides an `Authorization of (read.type | write.type)`
      without checking its scopes; within it, `read()` checks and narrows to
      `Authorization of read.type`:

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import internetAccess.online
      import logging.silentLogging

      given Authorizations = Authorizations()

      val issuer = Issuer
        ( url"https://issuer.example/token", t"client-1", t"s3cret",
          url"https://issuer.example/authorize", url"https://app.example/callback" )

      val read = Scope(t"read")
      val write = Scope(t"write")

      def handle(using Http.Request, Session): Http.Response =
        issuer.oauth:
          issuer.require(read, write):
            val checked: Authorization of read.type = read()
            Http.Response(Http.Ok)(t"may read")
      ```

      A scope should be a stable value (a `val` or an `object`), since its singleton type is
      what the refinement records; `Issuer#require` takes scopes this way.
  refer orthodoxy.Authorization
  refer orthodoxy.require
  entry #names   the scope names, as the issuer spells them
  entry #apply   checks the given authorization grants every scope, and returns it refined to this scope
      Needs an `Authorization of (? >: this.type)` in scope — an authorization refined to
      this scope or to a union including it, as `Issuer#require` provides; a plain
      `Authorization` is not one — and raises `OAuth.Error.Reason.InsufficientPrivileges(name)` for each name it does
      not grant. Unlike `Authorization#grants`, an authorization with no named scopes fails
      the check.
  entry #Check   the type of an authorization this scope can check: `Authorization of (? >: this.type)`

entry .Pkce   Proof Key for Code Exchange: a verifier the client keeps and the challenge it sends
  detail
      RFC 7636. A `Pkce` holds a random `#verifier`; its `#challenge`, the verifier's SHA-256
      digest in unpadded URL-safe base 64, goes with `Issuer#authorizationUrl`, and the
      verifier with `Issuer#exchangeCode`, so an authorization code intercepted on its way
      back cannot be exchanged by anyone without the verifier. A public client — one with no
      secret, such as a command-line tool or a mobile app — should always use one.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val pkce = Pkce()
      pkce.verifier.length   // 43
      Pkce(t"dBjftJeZ4CVP-mB92K27uhbUJU1p1r_wW1gFWFOEjXk").challenge
      // t"E9Melhoa2OwvFrEMTJguCHaoeK1t8URWbuGJSstw-cM"
      ```

      The same `Pkce` must be kept between the two steps of the flow.
  refer orthodoxy.Issuer
  entry .apply   a fresh verifier: 32 octets from a `SecureRandom`, as 43 URL-safe characters
  entry #verifier   the secret the client keeps and sends when exchanging the code
  entry #challenge   the `S256` challenge sent with the authorization request: the verifier's SHA-256, in URL-safe base 64

entry .Credential   a credential for a named security scheme, as `("name" is Credential to Result)`
  detail
      A typeclass keyed by the name an API's specification gives a security scheme, whose
      `Result` is the value the scheme calls for: `Text` for an API key, `Auth` for HTTP
      authentication, `Authorization` for an OAuth 2 token. A client which reads the
      specification — apoplexy's `Api` — finds the credential for each scheme an operation
      requires by name, checks its result fits the scheme's kind, and presents it as the
      scheme dictates: a header, a query parameter or a cookie.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import internetAccess.online
      import httpBackends.javaNetHttp
      import logging.silentLogging

      val issuer = Issuer(url"https://issuer.example/token", t"client-1", t"s3cret")

      given apiKey: ("api_key" is Credential to Text) = Credential(t"k-1")
      given petstore: ("petstore_auth" is Credential to Authorization) = issuer.clientCredentials()
      ```

      Name these givens: two anonymous givens of different credential types in one scope
      are synthesised with the same name and clash. A credential is keyed by the scheme's
      name alone, so two specifications which use the same name for different schemes must
      be given their credentials in separate scopes.
  refer orthodoxy.Authorization
  entry .Bound   the credential `Credential(value)` makes, for one scheme name and result type
    detail
        A `Credential` whose `Self` is the scheme's name and whose `Result` is the value's
        type; written as `"name" is Credential to Result`, it is what a given's declared type
        refines.
    entry #value   the credential's value
  entry .apply   makes a credential from its value, for the scheme name the expected type gives
  entry #Self   the scheme's name, as a literal `Label` type
  entry #value   the value the scheme presents: a key, an `Auth` or an `Authorization`

entry .OAuth   the home of `OAuth.Error`, raised whenever an OAuth exchange fails
  detail
      An object which holds `OAuth.Error` and its reasons.
  entry .Error   raised when an OAuth 2 grant, check or redirect fails
    detail
        Error 840: _OAuth failed because `reason`_, where the second number is the reason's.
        Every grant on `Issuer`, `Authorization.parse`, `Scope#apply` and the server
        middleware raise it, each through the `Tactic[OAuth.Error]` in scope; network,
        parsing and HTTP-status failures are all mitigated into it, so a caller handles one
        error type.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        capture[OAuth.Error](Authorization.parse(t"""{"token_type": "Bearer"}""".as[Json])).reason
        // OAuth.Error.Reason.InvalidJsonResponse
        ```
    entry .Reason   why an OAuth exchange failed, numbered as the second part of error 840
      detail
          A `Reason is Communicable` given in the companion renders each case:

          ```tabular
          column  Case
          column  Code
          column  Message
          column  Note
          row  `Connection(url, reason)`  1  _could not connect to the OAuth provider at `url` because `reason`_
          row  `InvalidJsonResponse`  2  _the token response was not valid JSON_
          row  `UnexpectedHttpStatus(status)`  3  _the provider returned an unexpected status: `status`_
          row  `InsufficientPrivileges(scope)`  4  _the user has not granted access to `scope`_
          row  `Unauthorized`  5  _authorization was not granted_  a `400` or `401` from the token endpoint
          row  `Other`  6  _an unexpected error occurred_  raised by the server middleware for a malformed or forged redirect
          row  `Misconfigured(detail)`  7  _the issuer is not configured for this: `detail`_  an `Issuer` lacks an endpoint a flow needs
          ```
      entry #number   the reason's number, the second part of the error's code
    entry #reason   why the exchange failed, as an `OAuth.Error.Reason`

entry .Authorizations   a web application's store of each session's authorization-code flow state
  detail
      The store `Issuer#oauth` and `Issuer#require` keep each `Session`'s
      `Authorizations.State` in, in `orthodoxy.server`. It is a thread-safe map held in
      memory, so it is lost when the server restarts, and is given once for the application:

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*

      given Authorizations = Authorizations()
      ```
  refer orthodoxy.oauth
  refer orthodoxy.require
  entry .State   one session's place in the flow: where the user was going, the `state` to check, the token
    detail
        Made by `Issuer#require` when it sends a user to the issuer, and completed by
        `Issuer#oauth` when the user returns with a code.
    entry #redirect   the path the user asked for, to which they are sent back once authorized
    entry #uuid   the random `state` the redirect back from the issuer must echo
    entry #access   the session's authorization once granted, or `Unset` while the flow is in progress
    entry #expired   whether the held authorization has expired; `false` while there is none
  entry #update   records a session's state, as `store(session) = state`
  entry #apply   the state recorded for a session, or `Unset`

entry .oauth   scintillate middleware which completes the authorization-code flow on the issuer's redirect
  detail
      An extension on `Issuer`, in `orthodoxy.server`. `issuer.oauth { … }` wraps a request
      handler: a request to any path but the issuer's `#redirect` runs the handler, with an
      `Issuer.Context of issuer.type` in scope for `require`. A request to the redirect path
      is the user returning from the issuer: its `state` parameter is checked against the
      session's `Authorizations.State`, its `code` is exchanged for an authorization (or, if
      the session holds an expired one with a refresh token, that is refreshed instead), the
      result is stored, and the user is redirected to the path they first asked for.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import internetAccess.online
      import logging.silentLogging

      given Authorizations = Authorizations()

      val issuer = Issuer
        ( url"https://issuer.example/token", t"client-1", t"s3cret",
          url"https://issuer.example/authorize", url"https://app.example/callback" )

      val read = Scope(t"read")

      def handle(using Http.Request, Session): Http.Response =
        issuer.oauth:
          issuer.require(read):
            Http.Response(Http.Ok)(t"authorized")
      ```

      It needs the `Http.Request`, an `Online` token, a `Http.Event is Loggable`, the
      `Authorizations` store, the request's `Session` and a `Tactic[OAuth.Error]`. A `state`
      which does not match is raised as `OAuth.Error.Reason.Other`, as is a malformed redirect;
      an issuer with no `#redirect` raises `Misconfigured`. The token request is made with
      the `java.net.http` backend whatever HTTP backend is in scope. A redirect for a session
      with no recorded state runs the handler.
  refer orthodoxy.require
  refer orthodoxy.Authorizations

entry .require   scintillate middleware which sends a user without an authorization to the issuer
  detail
      An extension on `Issuer`, in `orthodoxy.server`, used inside the same issuer's `oauth`
      (it requires an `Issuer.Context of issuer.type`). `issuer.require(scopes*) { … }` runs
      the handler with an `Authorization of scope` in scope when the session already holds
      an authorization; otherwise it records an `Authorizations.State` for the request's path
      and responds with a redirect to `Issuer#authorizationUrl`, asking for the names of all
      the scopes, with the state's UUID as `state`.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import internetAccess.online
      import logging.silentLogging

      given Authorizations = Authorizations()

      val issuer = Issuer
        ( url"https://issuer.example/token", t"client-1", t"s3cret",
          url"https://issuer.example/authorize", url"https://app.example/callback" )

      val read = Scope(t"read")

      def handle(using Http.Request, Session): Http.Response =
        issuer.oauth:
          issuer.require(read):
            Http.Response(Http.Ok)(t"authorized")
      ```

      The scopes must be stable values, such as `val`s, since their singleton types refine the
      authorization. The held authorization is refined to the scopes without checking them
      against its `#scopes`; a handler which must be sure calls `Scope#apply`. No PKCE
      challenge is sent.
  refer orthodoxy.oauth
  refer orthodoxy.Scope
